From: ginux-watch-agent <actions@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 12:24:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tests(devices): pin network view minor numbering and receive
 ring wrap order

### What
Pins two device behaviors: network `views` project major 0 with one-based minors (`slot index + 1`), and `IsolatedNetwork::receive` advances the ring head with modulo wrap so FIFO order survives a full wrap cycle.

### Why it matters
The minor number identifies the network interface to userspace; `index + 1` mutated to `index * 1` or `index - 1` shifts every interface id. The receive head `(head + 1) % FRAMES` mutated to `/` breaks wrap-around and returns stale frames. Existing view tests only cover pseudo-devices; existing receive tests never wrap the ring.

### Mutation evidence (fail-then-pass)
| Mutant | Existing suite | New test |
|---|---|---|
| `index as u32 + 1` -> `index as u32 * 1` (network minor) | PASS (survives) | FAIL (killed) |
| `index as u32 + 1` -> `index as u32 - 1` | PASS (survives) | FAIL (killed) |
| `(head + 1) % FRAMES` -> `(head + 1) / FRAMES` | PASS (survives) | FAIL (killed) |

Baseline (clean tree + new test): PASS. Reproduce: `cargo test -p ginux-devices --lib network_view_minor_is_one_based`.

Spec-Ref: device view projection contract (network minor numbering); ring-buffer FIFO semantics

Prepared with AI assistance; reviewed by me.
---
 crates/ginux-devices/src/lib.rs | 45 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 45 insertions(+)

diff --git a/crates/ginux-devices/src/lib.rs b/crates/ginux-devices/src/lib.rs
index 0dcdc474..479c6e88 100644
--- a/crates/ginux-devices/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/ginux-devices/src/lib.rs
@@ -989,4 +989,49 @@ mod tests {
         };
         assert!(!many.is_empty());
     }
+
+    #[test]
+    fn network_view_minor_is_one_based_and_receive_ring_wraps_in_order() {
+        let mut registry: DeviceRegistry<8, 8> = DeviceRegistry::new();
+        registry
+            .assign(
+                1,
+                30,
+                DeviceName::new(b"net0").unwrap(),
+                DeviceKind::Network,
+            )
+            .unwrap();
+        registry
+            .assign(
+                1,
+                31,
+                DeviceName::new(b"net1").unwrap(),
+                DeviceKind::Network,
+            )
+            .unwrap();
+        let pad = DeviceView {
+            name: DeviceName::new(b"pad").unwrap(),
+            kind: DeviceKind::Null,
+            major: 9,
+            minor: 9,
+        };
+        let mut out = [pad; 2];
+        assert_eq!(registry.views(1, &mut out), 2);
+        // network views: major 0, minor = slot index + 1 (one-based)
+        assert_eq!((out[0].major, out[0].minor), (0, 1));
+        assert_eq!((out[1].major, out[1].minor), (0, 2));
+        // ring wrap: fill 2, drain 1, refill 1 -> FIFO order preserved across wrap
+        let mut net: IsolatedNetwork<2> = IsolatedNetwork::new();
+        net.send(b"aa").unwrap();
+        net.send(b"bb").unwrap();
+        let mut buf = [0u8; 2];
+        assert_eq!(net.receive(&mut buf), Ok(2));
+        assert_eq!(&buf, b"aa");
+        net.send(b"cc").unwrap();
+        assert_eq!(net.receive(&mut buf), Ok(2));
+        assert_eq!(&buf, b"bb");
+        assert_eq!(net.receive(&mut buf), Ok(2));
+        assert_eq!(&buf, b"cc");
+        assert_eq!(net.receive(&mut buf), Err(DeviceError::QueueEmpty));
+    }
 }
